]:"}, {"text": "Pistol: Discuss unto me; art thou officer? Or art thou base, common and popular?", "reference": "1599 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Life of Henry the Fift”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act IV, scene i]:"}], "synonyms": ["apprise", "impart", "acquaint", "advise", "apprise", "discuss", "enlighten", "impart", "inform", "keep informed", "let know", "notify", "tell"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete, transitive) To break to pieces; to shatter.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["busticate", "fragment", "splinter", "toshiver"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete, transitive, colloquial) To deal with, in eating or drinking; consume.", "tags": ["colloquial", "obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "When the preparations were finished, he invited me with—“Now, sir, bring forward your chair.” And we all, including the rustic youth, drew round the table: an austere silence prevailing while we discussed our meal.", "reference": "1847 December, Ellis Bell [pseudonym; Emily Brontë], chapter II, in Wuthering Heights: […], volume (please specify |volume=I or II), London: Thomas Cautley Newby, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "We sat quietly down and discussed a cold fowl that we had brought with us.", "reference": "1854, Samuel White Baker, The Rifle and the Hound in Ceylon:"}, {"text": "In the first room we entered, a soldier and a man, like a clerk or dominie, were discussing a bottle of red wine; they immediately sprang up and politely proffered us each a bumper.", "reference": "1858, James Hogg, Titan, volume 27, page 306:"}], "synonyms": ["get outside", "put away", "consume", "discuss", "get outside", "ingest", "partake", "put away", "touch"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, law) To examine or search thoroughly; to exhaust a remedy against, as against a principal debtor before proceeding against the surety.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete, transitive) To drive away, disperse, shake off; said especially of tumors.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": ["June 15, 1751, Samuel Johnson, letter in The Rambler\nThe softness of my hands was secured by medicated gloves, and my bosom rubbed with a pomade prepared by my mother, of virtue to discuss pimples, and clear discolourations."], "quotes": [{"text": "For she was giuen all to fleshly lust, / And poured forth in sensuall delight, / That all regard of shame she had discust, / And meet respect of honour put to flight […]", "reference": "1590, Edmund Spenser, “Book III, Canto I”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "If too much milke be the cauſe, then the Nurſe ſhall not give the childe ſucke ſo often, nor in ſuch plenty: If it proceed from wind, and that doe cauſe the childe to be thus troubled, it ſhall be diſcuſſed with Fomentations applied to the belly and navell; and with Carminative Cliſters, which ſhall bee given him, […]", "reference": "1635, James Guillimeau [i.e., Jacques Guillemeau], “Of Gripings and Fretting in the Belly, which Trouble Little Children”, in The Nvrsing of Children. Wherein is Set Downe the Ordering and Government of Them from Their Birth. Together with the Meanes to Helpe and Free Them from All Such Diseases as may Happen unto Them. Written in French by Iames Guillimeau, the French Kings Chirurgion in Ordinary, London: Printed by Anne Griffin, for Ioyce Norton, and Richard Whitaker; published in Child-birth, or, The Happy Delivery of VVomen. VVherein is Set Downe the Government of Women. In the Time of Their Breeding Childe: Of Their Travaile, both Naturall and Contrary to Nature: And of Their Lying in. Together with the Diseases, which Happen to VVomen in Those Times, and the Meanes to Helpe Them. To which is Added, a Treatise of the Diseases of Infants, and Young Children: With the Cure of Them, and also of the Small Pox. With a Treatise for the Nursing of Children. Written in French by Iames Gvillimeav the French Kings Chirurgion, London: Printed by Anne Griffih, for Ioyce Norton, and Richard Whitaker, 1635, →OCLC, page 52:"}, {"text": "Many arts were used to discuss the beginnings of new affliction.", "reference": "1642, Henry Wotton, A Short View of the Life and Death of George Villiers:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["bechat", "confer", "bespeak", "betalk", "chat", "discuss", "moot", "talk about", "talk over", "treat"], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/discuss", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}